title: ZOG and KMS, hypertext built from the 1970s at Carnegie Mellon, drew skg's line between what a frame contains and what it only links to; Grudin cites a ZOG system that a warship's crew would not keep up.
pid: f6ab1c93-dbbb-433a-864a-2eaf210f8712
body: |2-
  Read in Ed's second dream: Wikipedia's articles on ZOG and on KMS,
  whole (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZOG_(hypertext) and
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KMS_(hypertext)), and Grudin's one
  paragraph, which cites McCracken and Akscyn, "Experience with the ZOG
  human-computer interface system", 1984, which I did not read. In
  Grudin, a management system for a naval vessel, built over ten years
  "to help the Commanding Officer and his department heads", was
  replaced by one without management features, "due in part to the
  difficulty of getting everyone to use it". Wikipedia says ZOG ran
  the carrier USS Carl Vinson's shipboard network from 1983.
  I am unsure of one inference: that the ZOG system Grudin cites is
  the Carl Vinson one; he cites the ZOG paper but does not name the ship.
  What they share with skg, from the Wikipedia articles:
  - A ZOG frame's title line "summarizes the frame's contents", as a
    title here makes the note's claim.
  - A frame's options led to subordinate frames, "like subpoints in an
    outline"; its local pads led to "tangential points". That is
    containment against link, as 'docs/data-model-skg-extensions.org'
    draws it.
  - An option marked '-' "leads nowhere": a target not yet written,
    the distinction Cleo found in Ward Cunningham's wiki.
  - KMS, ZOG's commercial successor from 1981, was many-user from the
    start, kept each frame's history, let an author protect a frame
    "from inadvertent editing even by themselves", had no separate
    edit mode, and marked link-bearing annotations as outside the
    hierarchy, "especially" for programs reading it.
  Why KMS did not spread I did not find. To read next: Akscyn,
  McCracken and Yoder, "KMS: a distributed hypermedia system for
  managing knowledge in organizations", CACM 31(7), 1988; I found no
  copy whole online.
